      "math_academy_id": "q-184533",
      "sequence_position": 5,
      "problem": "Let $I=[0,2]⊂R$ and ${X}_{i}=[-i,i]\\times [-1,1].$ Which of the following represents ${⋂}_{i\\in I}{X}_{i}?$",
      "worked_solution": "Recall that the intersection of a family of indexed sets consists of those elements that belong to *all* the sets:\n\n$$\n{⋂}_{i\\in I}{A}_{i}=\\{x\\,:\\,x\\in {A}_{k}\\,\\,\\text{for all}\\,\\,k\\in I\\}\n$$\n\nLet's now write down one of our indexed sets. For example, if $i=1,$ we have \n\n$$\n{X}_{1}=[-1,1]\\times [-1,1]=\\{(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in [-1,1],b\\in [-1,1]\\}.\n$$\n\nThis represents a rectangle whose vertices are at $(-1,-1),$ $(-1,1),$ $(1,1),$ and $(1,-1)$ in the coordinate plane, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14130949/assets/712970edb9966d6544cbe4a360aaa0de3436f356c4c7f23ff601c70ef168a521.png)\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=[-i,i]\\times [-1,1]=\\{(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in [-i,i],b\\in [-1,1]\\}\n$$\n\nis the rectangle whose vertices are at $(-i,-1),$ $(-i,1),$ $(i,1),$ and $(i,-1).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14130949/assets/68f8c51062bd9d3ecadd33b0a67ea0990f631375aba9f9cc705946c79abf47d0.png)\n\nThe intersection of all these rectangles is the line segment $\\{0\\}\\times [-1,1]:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14130949/assets/ffaa9807fa5155dcc848d68bed93a16b267045fae3f5d4d0945e064ad5c11eef.png)",
